---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SubjectGallery from '../../components/SubjectGallery.astro';

const title = 'Dragon Papercraft Designs: Fire, Ice & Low Poly';
const description = 'Free AI dragon papercraft designs — fire dragons, ice dragons and low poly versions. Copy a prompt, change the wings and color, and make your own.';

const siblings = [
  { href: '/gallery/animal/', label: 'Animals' },
  { href: '/gallery/dragon/', label: 'Dragons' },
  { href: '/gallery/vehicle/', label: 'Vehicles' },
  { href: '/gallery/building/', label: 'Buildings' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogType="website">
  <section class="hero container" style="padding-bottom:10px">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/gallery/">Gallery</a><span>/</span><span>Dragons</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="reveal">Dragon Papercraft Designs</h1>
    <p class="reveal" style="max-width:720px">
      Dragons are the hardest subject to get right and the most rewarding when you do. Wings need to look
      attached rather than stuck on, the tail has to carry a curve without sagging, and scales have to read
      as geometry rather than noise. In papercraft the low poly treatment solves most of this at once:
      faceted wings catch light, and a long body built from flat plates stays rigid.
    </p>
    <p class="reveal" style="max-width:720px">
      The designs below cover the two broad directions — fire and ice — plus low poly takes that strip a
      dragon down to its planes. Open any design for the exact prompt, then push it further: change the
      element, the wingspan, or the pose. If you want the faceted look specifically, start from the
      <a href="/tools/lowpoly/">low poly papercraft generator</a>; for scale detail and glow, use the
      <a href="/tools/fantasy/">fantasy generator</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <SubjectGallery subject="dragon" heading="Dragon papercraft designs" intro="" siblings={siblings} />
</BaseLayout>
